    "sample": "The tables show rising digital use alongside a smaller increase in supported access. Online applications grew from 400 to 900, whereas help-desk visits rose from 100 to 140. Average online waiting time fell, but help-desk waiting increased. These observations suggest that the channels experienced different pressures. They do not establish why residents chose a particular channel or whether the same people used both. Before reducing in-person support, the city should examine staffing, demographic patterns, and unresolved applications. A reasonable next step is to test additional assistance and compare results across both channels. The increase in online applications is much larger in absolute terms, but the help desk also handles more applications than before. This makes it inappropriate to describe in-person demand as disappearing. The waiting-time figures further suggest that growth has not been accompanied by equal improvements in both channels. While online waits halved, help-desk waits increased by fifteen minutes.\n\nSeveral explanations are possible. Staffing may have remained unchanged, or the cases arriving at the desk may have become more complex. The tables provide no evidence that allows us to choose confidently between these possibilities. They also do not reveal whether applications were completed successfully or how satisfied residents were with the service.\n\nThe next evaluation should therefore combine operational records with interviews. It should compare case complexity, completion rates, and staffing levels over similar periods. Until that evidence is available, retaining assistance while testing additional support is more defensible than assuming that online growth removes the need for human help.",
